# The maker lays `tag` and `turns` on the object that it creates. `_label` and `_first` are laid late, through `this`, by the setter of the literal that stands above: nobody lays them at birth, so the first read in the life of
# every object finds the slot empty. A read behind a test for a filled slot would fail there for ever and take the rest of the body with it: the read takes the slot that may be empty, behind the guard that nothing above answers.
# RUN: walk f4 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f4:Body
# CHECK: AreMembersUnchanged conditions=[{layout=L2 key="_label"}
# CHECK: = GetSlotOrUndefined {{*}}key="_label" receivers=[L2]
# CHECK: PutSlotOrAdd {{*}}key="_label" receivers=[L2]
# RUN(FILLED): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# FILLED-NOT: IsSlotFilled {{*}}key="_label"
# What the maker lays is likely there, as ever.
# RUN(MAKER): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# MAKER: = GetSlot {{*}}key="turns" receivers=[L2]
# The literal may be `this` of its own setter. It takes no slot under a name that is laid late on what stands below it: an empty slot on the way up may be filled by compiled code without a word to the run time, and the guard
# could never begin (ChainWalkUse::KeptForReads). A store on the literal itself lays an extra property, which the run time reports.
# RUN(FIELDS): link fields held mechanisms=all
# FIELDS-NOT: field L1.{{*}} "_label"
# FIELDS-NOT: field L1.{{*}} "_first"
# FIELDS: field L2.{{*}} "_label" appended {{*}}laid-late
# FIELDS: field L2.{{*}} "_first" appended {{*}}laid-late
# RUN(BIRTH): link fields held mechanisms=all
# BIRTH-NOT: "tag" appended {{*}}laid-late
# BIRTH-NOT: "turns" appended {{*}}laid-late
# RUN(ANSWERS): run-in-shell
# ANSWERS: the source prints the same
